The '''politics of [[Burawa]]''' are conducted within the framework of a {{W|socialism|socialist}} {{W|semi-presidential republic}} as established by the [[Constitution of Burawa|Constitution]] of 1932. The country's political system is characterised by a tripartite executive structure comprising the [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]], the [[President of Burawa]], and the [[Prime Minister of Burawa]], each with constitutionally distinct domains of authority. Legislative power is vested in a bicameral [[Parliament of Burawa|Parliament]] consisting of the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]] and the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]]. Judicial review is exercised by the [[High Court of Burawa]].

Burawa is constitutionally defined as a socialist state. All political parties that participate in the democratic process are required to adhere to the core tenets of the Constitution, which guarantee the rights of all citizens to {{W|healthcare}}, [[Housing in Burawa|housing]], food, happiness, equality, and privacy, provided by the state without discrimination on the basis of colour, class, or creed. Parties that do not uphold these principles are constitutionally prohibited from participation in elections. Within this framework, a diverse multi-party system has developed, encompassing a broad range of ideological positions on social policy, trade, foreign affairs, and the degree of permissible {{W|economic liberalisation}}.

== Constitution ==

The [[Constitution of Burawa]] was adopted in 1932 following the [[socialist revolution]] led by [[Maana Kadar]] and the [[Northern and Central Socialist Party]] (NCSP). The Constitution replaced the governmental framework of the [[First Republic of Burawa]] and established Burawa as a socialist republic. It remains in force, having been amended on numerous occasions to accommodate political and economic developments.

The Constitution enshrines several foundational principles. Burawa is defined as a socialist state in which the rights to healthcare, housing, food, happiness, equality, and privacy are guaranteed to all citizens. The state is obligated to provide these rights without bias, and all citizens are required to contribute to the collective systems that sustain them. These principles form the constitutional floor beneath which no legislation or executive action may fall, and they serve as the basis for determining the eligibility of political parties to participate in the democratic process.

Matters not directly addressed by the Constitution are governed by [[Act of Parliament (Burawa)|acts of parliament]]. Acts are the principal legislative instrument for implementing constitutional guarantees. For example, while the right to housing is enshrined in the Constitution, the mechanisms of the [[state housing system]] are defined by acts such as the [[Administration Reorganization Act]] of 1967. Acts require a simple parliamentary majority and the assent of the [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]], who possesses the power of {{W|veto}}.

=== Amendment process ===

Constitutional amendments require either a joint vote of sixty per cent of Parliament or a joint vote of fifty per cent of Parliament together with the consent of both the [[President of Burawa|President]] and the [[Leader of the Burawi Republic|Leader]]. This dual threshold ensures that amendments to the foundational law of the republic reflect either a broad legislative consensus or the agreement of both executive offices alongside a parliamentary majority.

== Executive branch ==

The executive authority of Burawa is divided among three offices: the Leader of the Burawi Republic, the President of Burawa, and the Prime Minister of Burawa. Each office exercises authority within a constitutionally defined domain, and the separation of these domains is a distinctive feature of the Burawi system of government.

=== Leader of the Burawi Republic ===

The [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] (commonly abbreviated '''LBP''', from the [[Burawi language|Burawi]] title) is the {{W|head of state}} and {{W|head of government}} of Burawa. The Leader is elected by direct popular vote for a five-year term and is not subject to {{W|term limit|term limits}}.

The office was created by the 1932 Constitution to serve as a civilian commander of the armed forces and as the chief {{W|political vanguard|vanguard}} of socialism in the republic. The establishment of the office reflected the influence of the [[Northern and Central Socialist Party]], whose {{W|vanguardism|vanguardist}} faction held a coalition majority during the constitutional convention and secured the inclusion of an elected executive with authority over the military, independent of the parliamentary leadership.

The Leader exercises authority over the [[Burawi Armed Forces]], [[Public agencies of Burawa|public agencies]] not administered by core ministries, and state-owned enterprises. Military administration falls entirely under the Leader's office, with a [[Joint Generals Consortium]] comprising the senior commanders of the [[Burawi Army]], [[Burawi Navy]], [[Burawi Air Force]], and [[Burawi Special Forces]] reporting directly to the Leader. The Leader may establish new public agencies — such as the [[Burawa Corrections Agency]], the [[Foreign Trade Agency]], and the [[Banking Administration Agency]] — subject to parliamentary confirmation. Public agencies may include {{W|intelligence agency|intelligence agencies}} and other bodies that operate under the direct purview of the office. The Leader may issue executive directives within these domains but is constitutionally prohibited from legislating. The Leader possesses the power to {{W|veto}} acts of parliament, which may be overridden by a joint vote of sixty per cent of both houses of Parliament or by a joint vote of fifty per cent together with the concurrence of the [[President of Burawa|President]]. The Leader does not possess the power to dissolve Parliament or call early elections; this authority was removed during the post-[[1962 Crisis]] reforms. The Leader may be recalled from office by a joint vote of seventy-five per cent of Parliament. In the event of a foreign invasion, the Constitution provides for the declaration of a {{W|state of emergency}}, which temporarily expands the Leader's executive authority.

The powers of the office were substantially reduced during the reforms of the 1960s, following the [[1962 Crisis]]. The contemporary role of the Leader as "chief vanguard of socialism" is regarded as largely symbolic, with the practical authority of the office concentrated in military command, the management of public agencies, and the exercise of the executive veto.

The Leader nominates the [[President of Burawa|President]], who must be confirmed by Parliament.

=== President of Burawa ===

The [[President of Burawa]] serves as the principal representative of the republic in {{W|international relations}}. The President is nominated by the Leader and confirmed by Parliament. The office has no fixed term; a President serves until a successor is nominated by the Leader and confirmed by Parliament. If Parliament declines to confirm a new nominee, the incumbent President remains in office. The President may be recalled by the Leader with the concurrence of sixty per cent of Parliament.

The President maintains an independent [[Office of Foreign Affairs (Burawa)|Office of Foreign Affairs]] and is responsible for representing Burawa at international summits, conferences, and diplomatic engagements. The President negotiates treaties and international agreements, which must be ratified by Parliament before taking effect. The Leader does not possess direct constitutional purview over international relations, although in practice the Leader and President collaborate in the formulation of foreign policy.

In the event that the President is recalled or incapacitated, the chief foreign officer of the [[Office of Foreign Affairs (Burawa)|Office of Foreign Affairs]] assumes interim responsibility for diplomatic functions until a successor is nominated and confirmed.

The President also serves a constitutional function in the legislative process. The President may join with a simple parliamentary majority (fifty per cent) to override a Leader's veto, providing a check on executive authority that is independent of the {{W|supermajority}} threshold. Because the President serves without a fixed term and remains in office until a successor is confirmed, a newly elected Leader may face a President nominated by a predecessor — particularly if Parliament declines to confirm the new Leader's nominee. This temporal misalignment functions as a constitutional safeguard, enabling the President to act as a moderating influence during transitions of executive power. The recall mechanism (Leader plus sixty per cent of Parliament) provides a means of removing a President in cases of severe misalignment, but the high threshold ensures that removal requires broad political consensus.

=== Relationship between the Leader and the President ===

The division of executive authority between the Leader and the President is designed to separate domestic governance from foreign representation. The Leader controls the military, public agencies, and the executive veto; the President controls diplomatic relations and the external representation of the state. In periods of political alignment — when the Leader and President are from the same party or coalition — the two offices operate in close coordination. In periods of misalignment, the President's independent authority over foreign affairs and role in the veto override mechanism may produce significant political tension.

As of 2025, the offices are held by members of different parties: [[Hancil Thea]] of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] serves as Leader, while [[Nacan Badalgen]] of the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]], nominated by former Leader [[Marik Sahamin]], continues to serve as President. This configuration has been widely noted in Burawi political commentary as a test of the constitutional separation of executive powers.

== Legislature ==

The [[Parliament of Burawa]] is the bicameral legislature of the republic, consisting of the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]] (upper house) and the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]]. The two chambers operate on separate electoral cycles and differ in their basis of representation.

=== Senate ===

The [[Senate of Burawa]] consists of '''230 seats''', each representing one [[Administrative divisions of Burawa|administration]]. Seats are apportioned as follows: 86 for [[independent urban administration]]s, 102 for [[dependent urban administration]]s, 8 for [[metropolitan administration]]s, and 34 for [[rural administration]]s. Senators are elected by direct vote within their respective administrations.

The entire Senate is renewed every '''three years'''. The most recent Senate election was held in 2024. Senators may be removed from office by {{W|recall election|popular recall}}, initiated by a petition bearing the signatures of two per cent of the administration's eligible voters.

=== Lower House ===

The [[Lower House of Burawa]] consists of '''796 seats'''. Each seat represents a single-member [[legislative district]] composed of one or more [[commune]]s. Legislative districts are apportioned on the basis of population, with each district comprising approximately 158,000 persons as of 2025. Since the 1990s, district boundaries have been determined by an {{W|algorithmic redistricting|algorithmic}} process that optimises for population density; prior to this, boundaries were drawn manually.

The entire Lower House is renewed every '''two years'''. Lower House elections were held concurrently with the [[2025 Burawi leadership election]]. Representatives may be removed from office by {{W|recall election|popular recall}}, initiated by a petition bearing the signatures of two per cent of the legislative district's eligible voters.

=== Legislative process ===

Legislation in Burawa takes the form of acts of parliament. Bills may be introduced in either the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]] or the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]]. An act requires a simple majority in both chambers and the assent of the [[Leader of the Burawi Republic|Leader]]. If the two chambers pass differing versions of a bill, a {{W|conference committee|joint reconciliation committee}} — composed of equal numbers of senators and Lower House representatives — is convened to produce a unified text, which is then resubmitted to both chambers for approval. If the Leader exercises the {{W|veto}}, Parliament may override the veto by a joint vote of sixty per cent of both houses, or by a joint vote of fifty per cent together with the concurrence of the [[President of Burawa|President]]. Joint votes are conducted as a combined tally of both chambers voting separately.

Constitutional amendments are subject to a higher threshold: either a joint vote of sixty per cent of Parliament or a joint vote of fifty per cent with the consent of both the President and the Leader.

== Prime Minister and Cabinet ==

The [[Prime Minister of Burawa]] is elected by the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]], typically from the largest party or coalition able to command a parliamentary majority. The Prime Minister may be removed by a {{W|vote of no confidence}}, in which case the Lower House must elect a replacement from among its existing members; a loss of confidence does not trigger new elections.

The Prime Minister is responsible for the management of legislation and the organisation and oversight of the '''core ministries''' of Burawa. Individual ministers are appointed by the Prime Minister and confirmed by Parliament. The core ministries are defined in the [[Constitution of Burawa|Constitution]] and correspond to the fundamental obligations of the state; adding or removing a ministry requires a constitutional amendment. The current core ministries are:

* [[Ministry of Health (Burawa)|Ministry of Health]]
* [[Ministry of Housing (Burawa)|Ministry of Housing]]
* [[Ministry of Education (Burawa)|Ministry of Education]]
* [[Ministry of Labour (Burawa)|Ministry of Labour]]
* [[Ministry of Humanity (Burawa)|Ministry of Humanity]] (civil rights enforcement, historic preservation, cultural protection and investment)
* [[Ministry of Agriculture (Burawa)|Ministry of Agriculture]]
* [[Ministry of the Treasury (Burawa)|Ministry of the Treasury]] (added during the post-[[1962 Crisis]] reforms)
* [[Ministry of Innovation (Burawa)|Ministry of Innovation]] (added in the 1990s)

Core ministries operate with institutional independence from the Leader; they are governed by their ministerial leadership and by the legislation that defines their mandates. The Leader may not issue directives to core ministries, ensuring a separation between the executive authority of the Leader and the administrative apparatus of the state. All other government functions outside the core ministries are administered by [[Public agencies of Burawa|public agencies]] under the authority of the Leader.

As of 2025, the Prime Minister is [[Iacob Naq]] of the [[Socialist Party of Burawa]], who leads a minority government in the Lower House.

== Judiciary ==

The judiciary of Burawa operates on three levels:

* '''[[Local courts of Burawa|Local courts]]''', organised at the administration level, serve as courts of first instance for civil and criminal matters.
* '''[[Appellate courts of Burawa|Appellate courts]]''', divided into geographic districts, hear appeals from the local courts.
* '''[[High Court of Burawa]]''', the highest judicial authority in the republic, exercises the power of {{W|judicial review|constitutional review}}.

The High Court may determine the constitutionality of acts of parliament and executive directives, and its rulings are binding on all branches of government. Constitutional challenges — including challenges to the eligibility of political parties — may be initiated by any citizen through the local courts and appealed through the appellate system to the High Court.

Judges of the High Court are selected from the lower courts and nominated by Parliament. The [[Leader of the Burawi Republic|Leader]] does not possess the power to veto judicial nominations, ensuring the independence of the judiciary from the executive.

== Local governance ==

=== Administrators ===

Each [[Administrative divisions of Burawa|administration]] is governed by an '''administrator''' (commonly referred to as a governor), who serves a fixed six-year term. Administrators are nominated by the senator representing the administration and confirmed by a vote of the [[commune]]s within the administration. The role is constitutionally {{W|nonpartisanship|nonpartisan}}; administrators are expected to serve as impartial executives of local government regardless of the political affiliations of their nominating senator, although in practice the appointment process frequently reflects partisan considerations. Administrators may be removed from office by {{W|recall election|popular recall}}; a petition bearing the signatures of two per cent of the administration's eligible voters triggers a recall election.

=== Community councils ===

Each [[community (Burawa)|community]] — a subdivision of a commune — is governed by a '''community council''' of three members elected directly by the residents of the community. There are currently 10,439 communities in Burawa.

=== Communal councils ===

At the commune level, Burawa operates a system of '''communal councils''' based on the principle of {{W|sortition}} — random selection from the eligible population. Each communal council represents approximately 10,000 residents, and most communes maintain approximately fifteen communal councils. Communal councils are distinct from the elected community councils that govern individual communities within a commune; the two bodies operate at different levels of the administrative hierarchy.

A communal council consists of seven members drawn at random from the eligible population of the commune. Eligible citizens must be of voting age, not currently incarcerated, and not serving in any existing elected office. Service on a communal council is {{W|mandatory service|mandatory}}; citizens who are selected may only be excused on grounds of incapacity or other valid reason, which must be presented before a court. Members serve a term of one year and receive a stipend for their service. Every six months, three members are rotated out and replaced by new randomly selected citizens, ensuring a continuous blend of experienced and incoming members. Councils are required to meet at least once per month.

Communal councils do not possess legislative authority. Their constitutional function is to serve as liaisons between residents and the commune's elected representatives, communicating local concerns, needs, and priorities to the formal governmental structure. In practice, the effectiveness of individual councils varies, as the random selection process produces bodies of differing levels of engagement. The system is nevertheless regarded as a significant institution of civic participation, providing ordinary citizens with direct experience of governance and a structured channel for community representation.

== Electoral system ==

=== Suffrage ===

All Burawi citizens aged eighteen years and older are eligible to vote. Voting is voluntary. Typical voter turnout in Burawi elections ranges between sixty and seventy per cent. The [[2025 Burawi leadership election]] recorded an unusually high turnout of approximately eighty-seven per cent, attributed to the exceptional political circumstances of the election; a comparable surge in participation was observed during the [[2015 Burawi general election|triple election of 2015]].

=== Election cycles ===

Burawa operates three independent electoral cycles:

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Office !! Cycle !! Last election !! Next election
|-
| [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] || 5 years || [[2025 Burawi leadership election|2025]] || 2030
|-
| [[Senate of Burawa]] || 3 years || 2024 || 2027
|-
| [[Lower House of Burawa]] || 2 years || 2025 || 2027
|}

Because the three cycles are of different lengths, the elections coincide at irregular intervals. A '''triple election''' — in which the Leadership, Senate, and Lower House are all contested simultaneously — occurs once every thirty years; the most recent was held in [[2015 Burawi leadership election|2015]]. The staggered cycles mean that a newly elected Leader may face a Senate or Lower House elected under different political conditions, producing periods of {{W|divided government}}.

=== Ranked-choice voting ===

{{W|Ranked-choice voting}} (RCV) has been used in [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]] elections since the 1960s, introduced by act of parliament as part of the post-[[1962 Crisis]] reforms. Under the RCV system, voters rank candidates in order of preference; the candidate with the fewest first-preference votes in each round is eliminated, and their ballots are redistributed according to voters' next-ranked preferences, until one candidate achieves a majority.

[[Senate of Burawa|Senate]] elections are conducted by {{W|first-past-the-post voting|first-past-the-post}} within each administration.

The [[2025 Burawi leadership election]] was the first '''leadership''' election conducted under RCV, adopted under the [[Leadership Election Reform Amendment]] endorsed by former Leader [[Marik Sahamin]]. The extension of RCV from the Lower House to the leadership race produced a result that would not have occurred under the previous first-past-the-post system, as the winning candidate, [[Hancil Thea]], placed second in first-preference votes but prevailed through the accumulation of second-preference support.

== Characteristics ==

The Burawi political system exhibits several structural features that distinguish it from other {{W|semi-presidential system|semi-presidential}} and {{W|socialist state|socialist}} governments.

=== Constitutional socialist pluralism ===

The constitutional requirement that all parties adhere to socialist principles does not, in practice, constrain the system to ideological uniformity. Thirteen parties currently hold parliamentary seats, spanning positions from {{W|anarcho-syndicalism}} to {{W|social conservatism}}, from {{W|free trade}} advocacy to {{W|protectionism}}, and from isolationism to international {{W|vanguardism}}. The constitutional floor — guaranteeing healthcare, housing, food, happiness, equality, and privacy as state obligations — functions as a minimum commitment to the {{W|welfare state}} rather than a prescription of specific economic or social policy. The breadth of the party system within these constraints is frequently cited in comparative political analysis as an example of pluralism within a constitutionally socialist framework.

=== Asymmetric executive authority ===

The Leader of the Burawi Republic possesses the power to {{W|veto}} legislation but is constitutionally prohibited from initiating it. This asymmetry concentrates the Leader's influence in the domains of obstruction and execution rather than creation. The Leader governs through directives to public agencies and state enterprises, and through the selective use of the veto, rather than through the legislative process. The core ministries — which administer the constitutional guarantees of healthcare, housing, education, and other services — are institutionally independent of the Leader and responsive only to parliamentary legislation and their own ministerial leadership. The effect is a dual domestic executive in which the Leader and the Prime Minister exercise authority over separate and largely non-overlapping institutional domains.

=== Separation of domestic and foreign authority ===

The constitutional assignment of {{W|international relations}} to the [[President of Burawa|President]] and domestic governance to the [[Leader of the Burawi Republic|Leader]] represents an unusual division among {{W|semi-presidential system|semi-presidential systems}}. In most comparable frameworks, the head of state retains significant authority over both domestic and foreign affairs. In Burawa, the two domains are constitutionally firewalled: the Leader has no direct purview over diplomacy, and the President has no direct authority over domestic policy. The arrangement ensures that the state's external commitments are managed by an office that is insulated from the domestic electoral cycle, since the President serves without a fixed term and is not subject to the same electoral pressures as the Leader.

=== Temporal checks on power ===

The Burawi system employs staggered electoral cycles as a structural constraint on rapid shifts in political direction. The three independent cycles — two years for the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]], three years for the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]], and five years for the [[Leader of the Burawi Republic|Leadership]] — ensure that no single election can produce a comprehensive change in government. A newly elected Leader inherits a Senate and Lower House that may reflect different political conditions. The [[President of Burawa|President]], who serves without a fixed term and can only be removed through the recall mechanism (Leader plus sixty per cent of Parliament), may remain in office indefinitely after a leadership transition if Parliament declines to confirm a successor. The design has the effect of distributing political mandates across time, requiring incoming governments to negotiate with institutional incumbents rather than governing by immediate majority. A triple election, in which all three legislative and executive cycles coincide, occurs only once every thirty years.

=== Geographic overrepresentation in the Senate ===

The [[Senate of Burawa]] apportions one seat to each of the 230 [[Administrative divisions of Burawa|administrations]], regardless of population. On a per-capita basis, this grants rural administrations significantly greater senatorial weight than urban ones: a rural administration with a population below 100,000 holds the same single seat as a dependent urban administration within the [[South Hudur]] metropolitan area, which contains over 18 million residents across 20 constituent administrations. However, the political effect of this imbalance is moderated by the overall composition of the Senate. Of the 230 seats, 110 belong to administrations within the eight [[metropolitan administration]]s (102 dependent urban plus 8 metropolitan), and many of the 86 independent urban administrations are substantial cities in their own right. Only 34 seats represent rural administrations. The result is a Senate in which urban and metropolitan interests hold a clear numerical majority, even as individual rural administrations enjoy disproportionate per-capita representation relative to their populations.

=== Algorithmic districting ===

Since the 1990s, the boundaries of the 796 [[legislative district]]s of the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]] have been determined by an {{W|algorithmic redistricting|algorithmic}} process that optimises for population density, replacing the manual boundary-drawing that had been practised since 1967. The system reduces the potential for {{W|gerrymandering}} by removing discretionary human input from the redistricting process.

=== The essential–non-essential boundary ===

The post-[[1962 Crisis]] constitutional amendments permit private enterprise in sectors classified by Parliament as "non-essential," while sectors deemed essential remain under state control. The classification is not fixed; Parliament may reclassify sectors through acts, and the boundary between essential and non-essential industries has shifted over time in response to economic conditions and political priorities.

As of 2025, sectors classified as '''essential''' — and therefore reserved for state operation — include public transit, aviation, {{W|pharmaceutical industry|pharmaceuticals}}, military production, {{W|nuclear power|nuclear energy}} production, construction, power transmission, {{W|extractive industry|mineral extraction}} (including petroleum and natural gas), and energy production (including {{W|hydropower}} and {{W|solar power}}). Aviation is subject to one exception: [[Mazar Airlines]], a private carrier that was {{W|grandfather clause|grandfathered}} prior to the current classification. Industries that directly implement constitutional guarantees — such as healthcare and education — are not subject to the essential/non-essential classification and are permanently reserved for the state.

Sectors classified as '''non-essential''' — and therefore open to private enterprise — include consumer services, retail, and hospitality. Certain industries have been reclassified over time: electronics manufacturing, {{W|semiconductor fabrication}}, and {{W|telecommunications}} were formerly classified as essential but have since been opened to private operation.

This mechanism provides a degree of economic flexibility within the socialist constitutional framework, allowing the scope of {{W|private sector|private industry}} to expand or contract without requiring constitutional amendment.

== Political parties ==

{{main|Political parties in Burawa}}

All political parties in Burawa are constitutionally required to adhere to the socialist principles of the [[Constitution of Burawa|Constitution]]. A party must uphold the state's obligations to guarantee the rights to healthcare, housing, food, happiness, equality, and privacy, provided without discrimination on the basis of colour, class, or creed, and must accept the principle that all citizens contribute to the collective systems that sustain these rights. Parties deemed inconsistent with these tenets are prohibited from participating in elections. Challenges to a party's constitutional eligibility may be brought before the [[High Court of Burawa|High Court]], which adjudicates whether a party's principles and conduct are consistent with the constitutional requirements.

Within this constitutional framework, a broad spectrum of political positions is represented. Parties range from socially conservative to progressive, from protectionist to free-trade, and from domestically focused to internationalist. The principal axes of ideological variation concern the degree of permissible {{W|economic liberalisation}}, the extent to which Burawa should promote socialism abroad, and the balance between metropolitan and rural interests.

